-- title:
--   Special fibre of the descended chart is integrally closed (q=2)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime with $q = 2$, $M'$ is a non-zero natural number with $q \nmid M'$, and $\ell_g$ is a prime with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$.
--
--   **Global reduction data.** $A$ is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$; $W$ is a finite set of places of the field `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` $= \kappa(A)(j(\mathsf q), j(\mathsf q^{M'}))$ over $\kappa(A) =$ `ResidueField A`, and `hW` says that $W$ consists exactly of the supersingular places `ssPlaces q M' (ResidueField A)`, i.e. those places $w$ that are rational (the structure map $\kappa(A) \to w$'s residue field is onto), lie on the affine $j$-locus, and have $w.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of the generator `jGeomGen` in the supersingular set `ssJSet q` (those $j$ for which every elliptic curve with that $j$-invariant has no non-trivial $q$-torsion point). The hypothesis `hle` records the inclusion `modularFunctionFieldBar M' ≤ fieldBar q M'` of the base-changed full modular function field of level $M'$ into that of level $\Gamma_{H(q,M')}(q^2M')$. $R_0$ is a `ConstantReduction` of $A$ on `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` with values in `modularFunctionFieldC (ResidueField A) M'` (a valuation subring of the source, a surjective residue map onto the target with kernel the maximal ideal, a map on places preserving degrees, compatibility with $A$ and with orders of functions), and `hR₀` asserts that for every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'`, that image lies in `R₀.integers` and its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\kappa(A)$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Finally $s$ is a chosen element of $W$.
--
--   **Small constants and the local base.** $k_0$ is an intermediate field of $\mathbb Q \subset \overline{\mathbb Q}$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ an element lying in $A$; write $A_0 =$ `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))` for the induced valuation subring of $k_0$. The hypotheses `hdvr`, `hunif`, `hhens`, `hres` say that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$, Henselian local, with algebraically closed residue field; `hκ` says that every $a \in A$ is congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to an element of $k_0$ lying in $A$.
--
--   **Auxiliary arithmetic constants.** A prime $\ell$ with $\ell \ge 3$, $\ell \ne q$, $\ell \nmid M'$; elements $\zeta_0, \varpi_t, \xi \in k_0$ with $\zeta_0$ a primitive $(q\ell)$-th root of unity in $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, $\xi$ a primitive $q$-th root of unity, $\varpi_t \in A$ and $\varpi_t^{q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$; an intermediate field $K_b$ of $k_0 \subset \overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $K_b = \bot$, a valuation subring $A_b$ of $K_b$ cut out by $A$ (`hAb`) and an element $\varpi_b \neq 0$ of $A_b$ generating its maximal ideal; and the tameness hypothesis `htame`, that the image of $(\ell_g-1)/2$ in $A_0$ is a unit.
--
--   **The rigid level field and its chart.** $H_1$ is the subgroup of $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ given by `hH₁` as the intersection of [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.levelH q M'`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelJacobian.html#L22) (the kernel of the unit-group reduction map attached to the divisibility `dvd_sq_mul q M'`) with the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$. $K_\ell$ is an intermediate field of $k_0$ in the Laurent series field over $k_0$, identified by `hKℓ` with `laurentBaseChange ↥k₀ (xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁)`, the $k_0$-span of the $q$-expansions of the modular functions of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$; it carries an $A_0$-algebra structure compatible with $k_0$. The element $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ is non-zero and, by `hjℓ`, equals the image of [`ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_X0.html#L157) under `coeffEmb`. Put $C =$ `chartAlgFin` $A_0\,K_\ell\,j_\ell$, the $A_0$-subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$. Further data: $\varpi_t' \in A_0$ with $\varpi_t' = \varpi_t$ in $k_0$; an ideal $y$ of $C$; an $A_0$-subalgebra $B_t \subseteq K_\ell$; a valuation subring $W_t$ of $K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ (`hBW`); natural numbers $n, m$; $\gamma_0 \in \mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$; a $k_0$-automorphism $\tau_0$ of $K_\ell$; an element $\zeta_c$ of the adic completion of $A_0$ at its maximal ideal; the membership `hjK` of [`ModularCurve.jqNModC ↥k₀ q`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L18) in $K_\ell$; an element $a_0 \in A_0$; and a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$.
--
--   Here and below, *level automorphism at $\gamma$* means the predicate [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ Kℓ τ`](def/ModularCurve_FullLevelLevelAutAt.html#L29): for all weights $k$, all modular forms $f, g$ of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ and weight $k$ with integral $q$-expansions, $g$ having non-zero $q$-expansion series, every $x \in K_\ell$ whose Laurent expansion is the image of the quotient of those series, and every embedding $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, the coefficientwise image of $\tau x$ multiplied by the $q$-expansion of $g \mid_k$ `conjElemN q γ` equals the $q$-expansion of $f \mid_k$ `conjElemN q γ`.
--
--   **The supersingular point $y$.** `hy`: $y$ is maximal; `hϖy`: the image of $\pi_0$ in $C$ lies in $y$; `hss`: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi : C \to \Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j_\ell)$ (via `jChartFin`) lies in [`ModularCurve.ssJSet q Ω`](def/ModularCurve_SupersingularModuli.html#L7). The hypothesis `hover` states that $y$ lies over the place $s$: for every $g$ in `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` whose base-changed image lies in `R₀.integers`, has non-negative order at every place of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` at which $j$ has non-negative order, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose residue in $\kappa(A)$ equals $s.\mathrm{evalAt}$ of that $R_0$-residue, the difference between $g$ (viewed in $C$) and $c$ belongs to $y$. The hypothesis `hOrbit` states that every maximal ideal $y'$ of $C$ containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same lying-over condition is the transport of $y$ along some level automorphism: there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a $k_0$-automorphism $\tau$ of $K_\ell$, a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$, preserving $C$, with $b \in y \iff \tau b \in y'$ for $b \in C$.
--
--   **The rigid chart axioms `hS1`** (a conjunction, all clauses listed): $C \subseteq B_t$; $K_\ell$ is the field of fractions of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$; $B_t/\pi_0 B_t$ has Krull dimension $\le 1$; $W_t \cap k_0 = A_0$; the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by the image of $\pi_0$; $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring; $y$ is the contraction to $C$ of the maximal ideal of $W_t$; $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ at that maximal ideal; for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure (`GaloisField q 2`) on the residue field $\kappa(A_0)$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B_t$ onto the Drinfeld coordinate ring [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q (ResidueField A₀)`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) (the quotient of $\kappa(A_0)[X_0,X_1]$ by `drinfeldPoly q ‹·› - 1`) whose zero set is exactly $B_t \cap \mathfrak m_{W_t}$, which is compatible with $A_0 \to \kappa(A_0)$, and which is equivariant: for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau$ a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ there is a unit $c$ of $\mathbb F_{q^2}$ with $(\mathrm{redQ}\,q\,\gamma, c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of the character $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho \circ \tau =$ `hAction` of that element composed with $\rho$, with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \neq \mathrm{id}$, and with a further $\gamma', \tau'$ realising the same $c$ with trivial matrix part; $B_t$ is stable under all level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$; every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ receives $\mathfrak m_{W_t}$ under some level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; and any $b \in B_t$ lying in $\mathfrak m_{W_t}$ after every such automorphism is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   **Inertia and width data.** $1 \le n$ and $n \mid q+1$; $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ with $\tau_0$ a level automorphism at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$; $1 \le m$ and $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t' w$ for a unit $w$ of $A_0$; $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$; the element `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` lies in $C$ (`hjC`) and its difference with $a_0$ lies in $y$ (`ha₀y`); and `hn`: $n =$ `placeWidthChar q M' s`, the quotient of `jWidthChar q` of the $j$-value of $s$ (at $q=2$: $12$ if that value is $0$, else $1$) by the ramification index `placeRamificationJ M' s`.
--
--   **The geometry of the special fibre.** The hypothesis `hR7` is a conjunction of twelve clauses, summarised here: $\tau_0^n$ acts trivially on $B_t$ while no smaller positive power does, and every level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with a power $\tau_0^k$, $k < n$; `ends` has exactly $q+1$ elements, the level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ permute `ends` transitively, $\tau_0$ preserves each end, and distinct ends are separated by an element that is a unit in one and not in the other; there is a finite-type, level-automorphism-stable $A_0$-subalgebra $B_c$ contained in every end, each end being a localisation of $B_c$; a level automorphism mapping some end into $W_t$ preserves $W_t$; each end $O$ satisfies $O \subseteq W_t$, is local and Noetherian, contains $C$, meets $k_0$ in $A_0$, has every element congruent to a constant, and admits a local model: an isomorphism $\iota$ of the adic completion of $O$ with the crossing model `UVCrossingModel` of the completion of $A_0$ at parameter $\pi_0^m$ (the quotient of a two-variable power series ring by $X_0X_1 - \pi_0^m$) carrying constants to constants, carrying two elements $c_x, c_y$ with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m \cdot (\text{unit})$ to unit multiples of $U$ and $V$ respectively with $c_y \in \mathfrak m_{W_t}$ and $c_x \notin \mathfrak m_{W_t}$, sending `jqNModC ↥k₀ q` $- a_0$ to a unit times $V^e$ with $e \ge 1$, conjugating $\tau_0$ into an automorphism $\theta_0$ with $\theta_0(U) \equiv \zeta_c U$ and $\theta_0(V) \equiv \zeta_c^{-1} V$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal, together with a branch valuation subring $W_x$ (dominating $O$, containing $C$, meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, a discrete valuation ring with uniformiser $\pi_0$, with the stated non-degeneracy of polynomials in a parameter and in `jqNModC ↥k₀ q`, detecting $y$, and distinguishing $O$ from the other ends) and a finite-type, stable subalgebra $B_x$ of $B_t \cap O$ with the stated integrality and localisation properties; finally, every valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ meeting $k_0$ in $A_0$, containing $C$, and whose maximal ideal contracts into $y$ either contains $B_t$ or dominates one of the ends. The hypotheses `hR9` and `hR10` provide elements of $B_t$ detecting non-dominance: `hR9` an element of $B_t$ lying outside every valuation subring $V$ with $V \cap k_0 = A_0$, $\pi_0$ in $\mathfrak m_V$ and $j_\ell$ generically non-integral in $V$; `hR10` such an element for every maximal ideal $y''$ of $C$ containing $\pi_0$ which is not the transport of $y$ along any level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$.
--
--   **The descent.** $G$ is the subgroup of $k_0$-automorphisms of $K_\ell$ generated by the level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$; $K_0$ is its fixed field; $B_0$ is the $A_0$-subalgebra of $K_\ell$ consisting (by `hB₀`) of the elements of $B_t$ fixed by every element of $G$; and $W_0'$ is the valuation subring of $K_0$ cut out by $W_t$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** The quotient ring $B_0/\pi_0B_0$, that is $B_0$ modulo the ideal generated by the image of $\pi_0$ under $A_0 \to B_0$, is integrally closed in the sense of `IsIntegrallyClosed`: every element of its fraction ring which is integral over it comes from the ring itself.
--
--   This is the normality statement for the special fibre of the chart obtained by descending the rigid supersingular chart along the deck group $\Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$, in the $q = 2$ case of the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ auxiliary-level frame used for the stable model of the modular curve; the invariants $B_0$ are analysed through their Drinfeld-curve quotient and the crossing model of each end. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.AuxLevel.formallySmooth_invariants_of_rigidChart_linkedScalars_of_eq_two_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.AuxLevel.formallySmooth_invariants_of_rigidChart_linkedScalars_of_eq_two_of_dvd), which establishes the corresponding smoothness property of the descended chart.
